    "sdAboutWhatTitle": "What is it?",
    "@sdAboutWhatTitle": {},
    "sdAboutWhatBody": "A one-tap diagnostic that finds the likely bottleneck between you and the internet — without you having to compare numbers across separate screens.",
    "@sdAboutWhatBody": {},
    "sdAboutHowTitle": "How does it work?",
    "@sdAboutHowTitle": {},
    "sdAboutHowBody": "Five short probes run end-to-end and the results are compared against published thresholds:",
    "@sdAboutHowBody": {},
    "sdAboutHowBullet1": "Signal — reads RSSI from the connected access point.",
    "@sdAboutHowBullet1": {},
    "sdAboutHowBullet2": "Channel — scores your channel against neighbouring APs.",
    "@sdAboutHowBullet2": {},
    "sdAboutHowBullet3": "Speed — runs a real download/upload test against Cloudflare.",
    "@sdAboutHowBullet3": {},
    "sdAboutHowBullet4": "Bufferbloat — measures latency under load (Waveform A–F).",
    "@sdAboutHowBullet4": {},
    "sdAboutHowBullet5": "DNS — benchmarks public resolvers vs. your current one.",
    "@sdAboutHowBullet5": {},
    "sdAboutCategoriesTitle": "What do the categories mean?",
    "@sdAboutCategoriesTitle": {},
    "sdAboutCategoriesBullet1": "Weak Signal — Wi-Fi link forced into slower modes by distance / walls.",
    "@sdAboutCategoriesBullet1": {},
    "sdAboutCategoriesBullet2": "Crowded Channel — neighbouring APs on the same channel eat your air-time.",
    "@sdAboutCategoriesBullet2": {},
    "sdAboutCategoriesBullet3": "Bufferbloat — latency balloons when the link is fully loaded; calls and games suffer.",
    "@sdAboutCategoriesBullet3": {},
    "sdAboutCategoriesBullet4": "ISP Slow — Wi-Fi is fine but your plan / upstream is the ceiling.",
    "@sdAboutCategoriesBullet4": {},
    "sdAboutCategoriesBullet5": "Slow DNS — page loads feel laggy because name lookups take too long.",
    "@sdAboutCategoriesBullet5": {},
    "sdAboutEstimateTitle": "About the speed-up estimate",
    "@sdAboutEstimateTitle": {},
    "sdAboutEstimateBody": "Each finding shows a conservative projected gain — what you can realistically expect after applying the fix. It is a lower bound, not a guarantee, and it depends on the test conditions.",
